projects.how_its_made:
The application is organized into four main layers: creator onboarding, AI generation and storage, agent identity, and machine-to-machine payments. Creator onboarding Creators authenticate with Privy, which provisions an embedded EVM wallet. From the dashboard, a creator connects an authorized URL, RSS feed, or uploaded document and defines the agent’s tone, target audience, output formats, content restrictions, approval settings, and payout address. For the hackathon prototype, we deliberately avoid training a separate model for every creator. Each agent uses: a shared model + per-author retrieval corpus + author profile + content policy + persistent memory This keeps agent creation fast while still producing author-specific behavior. 0G Compute and Storage 0G Compute is the inference layer. It processes the source content together with the author profile and generates structured outputs such as an X thread, newsletter summary, short social post, and citation bundle. 0G Storage is used for source snapshots, author configuration, generation metadata, approved outputs, provenance references, and evolving agent memory. A generation record links the output to its source identifier, prompt version, agent identity, and storage reference. ENS identity and discovery When an agent is created, the platform programmatically assigns it an ENS subname such as: alice-tech.publisher-agents.eth The name resolves to the agent wallet and stores discovery metadata through ENS text records. We use the ENS agent-record model to expose an agent-context and a web or API endpoint. This lets another application discover how to interact with the agent using only its ENS name. ENS is the identity and discovery root, but it does not self-assert reputation. The reputation shown on the agent profile is calculated from observable events such as: successful paid deliveries;
unique purchasers;
failed or refunded deliveries;
cumulative revenue;
provenance records;
repeat purchasers.
Hedera and x402 payments Premium content is exposed through an x402-protected endpoint. When a client requests a licensed bundle, the API returns HTTP 402 Payment Required with the amount, accepted asset, destination, and facilitator information. The consumer signs the value transfer, while the Hedera x402 facilitator acts as the transaction fee payer, verifies the payment, submits it to Hedera, and returns the settlement receipt. After confirmation, the API releases the content bundle and records the delivery against the agent’s reputation profile. For the MVP, payments are priced in native HBAR. This avoids introducing an additional HTS-token association flow and keeps the demonstration focused on one discrete request, one payment, and one content response. Prefunding extension The prefunding flow reuses the same payment infrastructure. A creator publishes several proposed topics, supporters make small x402 contributions, and Hedera receipts update each campaign total. When the target or deadline is reached, the selected topic moves into the 0G generation workflow. Supporter receipts can later be used to provide early access before the creator approves a public release. Technical Considerations:
The frontend provides Privy-based authentication, an embedded EVM wallet, creator onboarding, agent configuration, generation review, sales analytics, and prefunding campaign management. When a creator provisions an agent, the backend creates an author profile containing the authorized source, supported formats, tone, policy constraints, payout destination, and approval settings. Instead of fine-tuning one model per creator, the prototype combines a shared inference model with a creator-specific retrieval corpus and persistent configuration. The generation pipeline retrieves the authorized source and profile, invokes 0G Compute, validates the structured response, and uploads the source snapshot, output, prompt version, and provenance metadata to 0G Storage. The resulting storage identifiers are attached to the agent profile and generated bundle. The identity service creates an ENS subname for every agent. We publish an agent-context record describing the agent’s capabilities and an agent-endpoint[web] record pointing to its public profile or API. A client can therefore start with only an ENS name and discover the agent address, supported content formats, provenance references, payment endpoint, and reputation profile. Premium bundles are protected by an x402 middleware layer. An unpaid request returns 402 Payment Required. The consumer creates and signs the Hedera transfer authorization, and the facilitator verifies and settles the transaction as fee payer. Following settlement, the resource server returns the licensed bundle and records the transaction ID, buyer, delivery status, and revenue. The reputation service aggregates payment and delivery events rather than trusting a creator-editable ENS value. ENS points to the reputation profile, while Hedera settlement receipts and stored provenance provide the supporting evidence.

projects.project_description:
Catenor One is the first reference implementation of Catenor Protocol, an open protocol for canonical identity and verifiable authority for humans, organizations and agents. The problem: tokenized assets prove who holds them, not who may be paid today. A wallet is not an identity, a relationship is not a capability, and a validly signed credential is not current eligibility. Catenor gives every subject a persistent did:catenor identity that is independent of wallets and identity providers. Organizations become Trust Anchors through a confidential admission. Authority flows as explicit, scoped capabilities that can be delegated but never exceed the delegator's. Investors hold W3C Verifiable Credentials. Every payout is gated by a confidential decision about current eligibility. The live demo ran end to end on testnet (24/24 stages). A Trust Anchor is admitted by a deployed Chainlink Confidential Workflow and authorizes a Sponsor. The Sponsor tokenizes a synthetic real-estate SPV on Hedera Asset Tokenization Studio and issues 600 and 400 units to two investors whose KYC passed in the Sumsub sandbox. A dividend is declared, with entitlements of 6 and 4. Then one investor is sanctioned. He still holds his 400 units and a valid credential, but the TEE re-checks today's evidence and decides HOLD. A Privy-controlled Distribution Agent, authorized only through delegation, pays the eligible investor 6 HBAR and never even requests a signature for the other. Every DENY path is part of the demo: a capability used outside its scope;
an agent with a wallet but no capability;
a relationship without a capability;
a non-delegable action.
Everything is verifiable: the CRE execution IDs, the HashScan transactions, the Privy policies and a hash-chained audit log. Honest labels: the Sumsub sandbox uses synthetic applicants, the company KYB in the admission is a synthetic mock, and Hedera runs on testnet.

projects.how_its_made:
K01 separates a protocol layer (engine/) from an application layer (app/). The layering is enforced in CI (pnpm check:layering), so the protocol stays publishable on npm as @knowledge01/* and never depends on any UI. Key technologies and architecture Identity and access control (ENS v2 on Sepolia). ENS v2 is both the naming system and the access controller. Every namespace is an ENS name, and its contenthash points at the namespace's current state on IPFS. Each namespace has its own PermissionedResolver, so ENS enforces who can write and no backend decides it: the owner holds the root roles;
a designated reviewer is granted ROLE_SET_CONTENTHASH on that name, so they can publish what they land;
a named contributor is granted ROLE_SET_DATA on a single key, knowledge.proposal.<name>, where they post a pointer to their proposal for review and can write nothing else. The access manifest (offers and sealed grants) is the knowledge.access text record on the same name, and anyone can resolve it through the Universal Resolver. Versioned content storage (IPFS via Pinata). Claims, commits and the refs object (branches, policy, proposals) form a Git-like, content-addressed graph on IPFS, pinned through Pinata. Every object is identified by its hash, and a reader checks each hash on retrieval, so any tampering is detected. On-chain data (MultiBaas, Blockscout, DefiLlama). The main demo, Portfolio Intelligence, tracks market signals and wallet activity on Base. MultiBaas reads every wallet balance on Base mainnet: ETH from its address endpoint, and ERC-20 tokens by calling balanceOf through its built-in ERC-20 interface. So we run no RPC node and maintain no index. Agent A reads the watched whale wallets this way and writes what it finds as sourced, reviewed claims. Agent B reads the user's own wallets the same way. We cache the reads to stay within the free plan. Blockscout supplies transfer history, and DefiLlama supplies prices and pool yields. Payments between agents (x402, USDC on Base Sepolia). The paid tier, signals.treasury.eth, is encrypted with AES-256-GCM and sells read access over x402 for $0.01 a week. When a question needs that data and the agent holds no live grant, the model decides whether to buy. It pays in USDC on Base Sepolia. Once the payment settles, the namespace key is sealed to the buyer's public key with ECIES, and the grant is published to ENS in knowledge.access. The agent then reads the paid tier with its own key. Developer tools. Agents and developers use K01 through: an MCP server, @knowledge01/mcp, with 19 knowledge_* tools;
a TypeScript SDK, @knowledge01/repo;
a CLI, @knowledge01/cli, whose command is knowledge;
a ready-made skill for Claude Code and rule for Cursor, so coding agents read a team's conventions namespace.

projects.how_its_made_markdown:
K01 separates a protocol layer (engine/) from an application layer (app/). The layering is enforced in CI (pnpm check:layering), so the protocol stays publishable on npm as @knowledge01/* and never depends on any UI.

Key technologies and architecture

Identity and access control (ENS v2 on Sepolia). ENS v2 is both the naming system and the access controller. Every namespace is an ENS name, and its contenthash points at the namespace's current state on IPFS. Each namespace has its own PermissionedResolver, so ENS enforces who can write and no backend decides it:

> the owner holds the root roles;
> a designated reviewer is granted ROLE_SET_CONTENTHASH on that name, so they can publish what they land;
> a named contributor is granted ROLE_SET_DATA on a single key, knowledge.proposal.<name>, where they post a pointer to their proposal for review and can write nothing else.

The access manifest (offers and sealed grants) is the knowledge.access text record on the same name, and anyone can resolve it through the Universal Resolver.

Versioned content storage (IPFS via Pinata). Claims, commits and the refs object (branches, policy, proposals) form a Git-like, content-addressed graph on IPFS, pinned through Pinata. Every object is identified by its hash, and a reader checks each hash on retrieval, so any tampering is detected.

On-chain data (MultiBaas, Blockscout, DefiLlama). The main demo, Portfolio Intelligence, tracks market signals and wallet activity on Base. MultiBaas reads every wallet balance on Base mainnet: ETH from its address endpoint, and ERC-20 tokens by calling balanceOf through its built-in ERC-20 interface. So we run no RPC node and maintain no index. Agent A reads the watched whale wallets this way and writes what it finds as sourced, reviewed claims. Agent B reads the user's own wallets the same way. We cache the reads to stay within the free plan. Blockscout supplies transfer history, and DefiLlama supplies prices and pool yields.

Payments between agents (x402, USDC on Base Sepolia). The paid tier, signals.treasury.eth, is encrypted with AES-256-GCM and sells read access over x402 for $0.01 a week. When a question needs that data and the agent holds no live grant, the model decides whether to buy. It pays in USDC on Base Sepolia. Once the payment settles, the namespace key is sealed to the buyer's public key with ECIES, and the grant is published to ENS in knowledge.access. The agent then reads the paid tier with its own key.

Developer tools. Agents and developers use K01 through:

> an MCP server, @knowledge01/mcp, with 19 knowledge_* tools;
> a TypeScript SDK, @knowledge01/repo;
> a CLI, @knowledge01/cli, whose command is knowledge;
> a ready-made skill for Claude Code and rule for Cursor, so coding agents read a team's conventions namespace.

projects.project_description:
Phinary is a prediction market built from Uniswap pools, where betting on binary positions is just a token swap. There are no external oracles, off-chain APIs or order books: it runs entirely on Uniswap pools, and everything a market needs is calculated inside Uniswap v4 hooks. Each market asks one question, such as "Will ETH be above $2,689.27 at 22:45?", and has two ERC-20 tokens, UP and DOWN, each trading against USDC in its own v4 pool. At expiry the winning token redeems for exactly 1 USDC and the losing one for nothing. Four tracks run on one hook and one vault: ETH and SOL, each with 1-minute and 15-minute markets. Pricing with Black–Scholes: The fair price of UP is the probability that the price ends above the strike. PredictionHook computes it on every swap with the Black–Scholes formula for a binary option. All three inputs come from Uniswap itself: S, the underlying pool's price at the start of the current block;
K, the strike, which is the pool's price when the market opened;
σ, the volatility, which the oracle hook measures from the same pool's price history over the last 30 minutes.
A market settles on the average price over its final 10 or 30 seconds, not on a single instant, so the hook prices exactly that: UP price = Φ( (ln(S/K) − ½σ²·T_D) / (σ·√T_V) ) Φ is the normal distribution. T_D and T_V are the time to expiry, adjusted for averaging over the settlement window. With no window, this is the textbook binary option price N(d₂). DOWN is priced as the mirror image of UP. We wanted to make a new financial product available through infrastructure traders already use. Phinary puts option pricing and settlement inside a Uniswap v4 hook, with UP and DOWN　positions represented as tradable tokens. Our demo uses a fork of the Uniswap interface to show the full journey from purchase to payout. Liquidity providers fund the other side. They deposit USDC into a shared vault through the Phinary dashboard and receive shares representing their stake. That vault backs multiple option markets. Its value changes with trading spreads and settlement payouts, so providing liquidity carries risk as well as the opportunity to earn returns. The current deployment runs on Unichain Sepolia, with one-minute and fifteen-minute options for both ETH and SOL. We also built a TypeScript SDK so other applications can quote and execute trades against the same contracts.

Ticket: options trading that feels like trading perps Options are powerful but hard to use. Most options UIs are a grid of strikes and expiries, and you have to picture the payoff in your head. Ticket turns the price chart itself into the order ticket, so building an options position feels as direct as opening a perp. How it works The screen is a large price chart. Past price shows as candles; the future side shows every listed expiry. Pick Buy/Sell Call or Put and click anywhere in the future: a leg is placed at the nearest listed strike and expiry. The whole future area is shaded as a P&L heat map over time and price: green where the position makes money, red where it loses, and brighter for bigger P&L. On top of the map, Ticket draws: a break-even line;
outlines where P&L is within 5% of the max profit or max loss, traced from the exact P&L right up to expiry;
hatching and tags when a loss (or profit) is uncapped. Legs can be dragged, and the map morphs smoothly as you move them. A handle between legs drags a whole structure at once (e.g. slide a call spread to a new strike and date while keeping its width). Compounding and tracking Stack any number of legs into spreads, condors, calendars or custom shapes. A sidebar shows: strategy name, net debit/credit, max profit/loss, break-evens, chance of profit and greeks;
presets for common structures, plus full undo and redo. A Positions tab tracks open positions with live P&L, a P&L sparkline, and progress toward max profit. You can tick several positions to see their combined exposure on the chart together with a new trade. Draw path Draw where you think price will go, and Ticket builds the position that pays off along that path. It places a call butterfly on each listed expiry your line crosses, centred on your line's price at that date, with wings sized to the expected move. It then shows what you'd make if price follows your line. Built on Derive Live mainnet data: listed expiries and strikes, index price, price history, and each option's mark, bid, ask and implied volatility. Ticket prices with the implied volatility that reproduces Derive's mark, so the map matches exchange prices.
Testnet trading on Derive's v3 API (Sepolia): connect with MetaMask, sign in, and authorise a scoped, 7-day trading key created in the browser. That key can trade but not withdraw, and your wallet key never leaves MetaMask.
Order review: every leg gets a dry-run quote from Derive (validity, expected fill, fees) before signed orders are sent, as market or limit-at-mark. Your Derive positions and open orders appear in the app, and you can close or cancel them.
Builder code: every order carries a Derive builder code and fee. A demo mode with simulated data works anywhere. Transparency AI use: the code was written with Claude Code, working from my direction; every commit is co-authored. I contributed the product concept, feature and design decisions, hands-on testing and bug reports, the Derive and MetaMask setup, and the deployment.
Earlier start: an early UI prototype was started shortly before the official start, and recreated in this repo in the first 9 commits. Everything after that was built during the event.

projects.how_its_made_markdown:
Contracts are Solidity with Foundry (26 tests). PQVault holds the three guardians and does validate() for both VERIFY frames and execute() for SENDER. PQVaultFactory deploys accounts with CREATE2. LeafLedger stores the per-slot high-water marks and accepts equivocation reports. PQVaultFrameOps is a Yul helper for the EIP-8141 opcodes (TXPARAM, SIGPARAM, SIGDATACOPY, FRAMEPARAM, APPROVE). Signatures go in ARBITRARY signature entries and are checked against the protocol's canonical signature hash. We wrote no cryptography of our own: verification is upstream JARDIN's FORS and SPX verifiers, unmodified, and signing is JARDIN's Python reference signers, pinned as a git submodule.
 Partner tech: we built on the ETHGlobal Mumbai alt-mempool workshop stack, namely forshtat's ethrex fork with EIP-8141 and a tracer, the hegota-support helpers and the skandha-based mempool sidecar. Two signature verifications cost more validation gas than EIP-8141's combined 100k cap: about 84k per VERIFY frame for a compact signature, about 292k for a stateless one. Reading the shared LeafLedger is also "storage not owned by the sender", which the canonical mempool refuses. So we implemented ERC-8421 [BUDGET-010] (a separate 1M-gas validation budget per staked entity) as a patch to the workshop sidecar: 7 new tests, all 393 executor tests passing, and a Docker image. The vault stakes itself when it is created. We measured that the canonical mempool and the stock sidecar reject the transaction, and the patched sidecar admits the staked vault while rejecting an unstaked copy.
 On a local Kurtosis network (ethrex + Lighthouse + the patched sidecar), a payment uses VERIFY frames of 83,842 and 75,875 gas and a SENDER frame of 59,809. It is billed 379,513 gas, because frame transactions are charged by calldata size and post-quantum signatures are large. The first use of a key costs 97,920 more to create its ledger entry. The wallet's fee estimate reproduces 25 of the 26 frame-transaction receipts on the chain exactly (the one exception is a recovery transaction the wallet never sends).

 The off-chain toolkit is Python with no pip dependencies:
 - an EIP-8141 frame-transaction encoder, checked against ethrex's test vector;
 - a key store that records a leaf as used before signing with it (burn-before-sign);
 - a devnet driver for rotation, recovery and the attack;
 - the wallet servers and the frame-transaction explorer;
 - 44 unit tests.

 Hacky but notable:
 - Upstream JARDIN's hashing shares module-level buffers, so we serialise every call to it from our multi-threaded servers.
 - The phone app is Expo, running in Expo Go. It reaches the wallet over an ngrok tunnel, paired with a code. The wallet treats any forwarded request as remote, so the tunnel can't bypass pairing.
- The research side is a simulator with fault injection plus a crash test that kills the signer 100+ times.

OpenLabel has four parts: a React web app, a Python API, a Rust zero-knowledge prover, and Solidity contracts on Base.

Frontend: React 19 + Vite + TypeScript, with viem for all chain access. Users sign in with Google or email (Privy), MetaMask or WalletConnect. FastAPI on Postgres serves products and test requests, and existing results from public food-testing sites are imported so the catalogue is useful from day one. The app deploys to Render as one service: FastAPI serves the built frontend.

Proving lab reports: the tester uploads the lab's PDF. The prover reads the report link from the QR code printed on the PDF and fetches the lab's own copy from the lab's server under TLSNotary, which signs a record of what the lab's server returned. It then requires the upload to match that copy byte for byte. Inside an SP1 zkVM, a custom Rust PDF parser blanks out the customer's name and address and extracts the test results. The result is a Groth16 proof that shows three things together: the report came from the lab, it wasn't altered, and only the test values are made public. Proving runs on a GPU server, keeps going in the background while the user browses, and the tester then submits it on-chain with one click.

Contracts (Solidity, Foundry): EurekhaReportRegistry checks the proof, the notary's signature and the lab's domain. TestFund runs one pool per request:
- anyone contributes toward the lab cost;
- anyone can take the job by staking $110;
- a valid proof pays the tester the stake plus the pool, and any shortfall counts as the tester's own contribution;
- a tester who misses the 30-day deadline loses the stake to the pool, and the job reopens;
- the admin can later share revenue (e.g. affiliate income) among contributors in proportion to what each put in.

The proven results are decoded straight from the chain and shown on the product page, so anyone can check them without trusting our server.

projects.project_description_markdown:
Every Ethereum account today is secured by ECDSA, which a quantum computer can break. Qrypt is a wallet whose account has no ECDSA key at all: it's a smart account that only accepts Falcon-512 signatures, checked on-chain.

It runs on EIP-8141 frame transactions. One transaction verifies the Falcon signature, lets a paymaster check it'll get paid, repays the gas in USDC, then does whatever you asked. So a brand-new account holding only USDC can send, swap, and use dapps with zero ETH, and there's no bundler, relayer or paymaster server.

The catch: Falcon verification costs ~700k gas and the canonical mempool caps validation at 100k, so these transactions can't get in anywhere today. We implemented the ERC-8421 draft's per-entity budget (BUDGET-010) in the alt-mempool sidecar so staked accounts get up to 1M, sent it upstream as a PR, and it's what lands our transactions on the public devnet.

On top of that, it's a full wallet:
- send/receive any token;
- swaps on Uniswap v4;
- a Chrome extension with dapp connect (EIP-6963) and Sign-In verified onchain via ERC-1271;
- an installable phone app with Face ID unlock and Face ID recovery, QR pay links and push notifications;
- key rotation and encrypted backups.
New accounts are free, so World ID keeps it to one sponsored account per human.

Sableye is Stripe for stablecoins: a drop-in SDK that lets any business accept USDT on Whitechain without ever handing its money to a middleman.

The problem. Businesses that want to take crypto payments today have two bad options. A custodial gateway holds their money and can freeze it. Or they paste a wallet address and check an explorer by hand: no invoices, no refunds, no receipts, no accounting. Sableye gives them the full Stripe experience (invoices, a checkout widget, refunds, subscriptions, webhooks, emails, a dashboard) while every payment goes straight from the customer's wallet to the merchant's wallet. Sableye never holds funds.

How it works. The merchant's server signs an EIP-712 invoice with its own key: amount, token, order ID, expiry, payout address. Signing costs no gas and needs no transaction. The customer pays it through our PaymentProcessor contract, which checks the merchant's signature, the expiry and the exact amount, and moves USDT directly to the merchant. Each invoice can be paid only once, refunds are capped on-chain at what was paid, and the contract never holds tokens. The checkout picks the best payment path automatically:
- gasless: EIP-3009, where the customer holds no gas token and the merchant's server pays a fraction of a cent;
- permit: one transaction;
- approve: works with any token.

The server confirms each payment from the chain before fulfilment, never from the browser.

What a merchant gets (one npm package: npm install sableye):
- invoices and payment links with QR codes;
- a styled <Checkout> component with themes, plus a one-line <script> drop-in for plain HTML sites;
- mobile wallets via WalletConnect;
- gasless payments;
- full and partial refunds;
- recurring subscriptions with a customer-approved spending cap, charged automa keeper;
- coupons;
- signed webhooks with replay;
- email invoices, receipts and "you got paid" notices with PDFs;
- revenue analytics (MRR, churn), reconciliation and CSV export.

Built for AI coding agents. npx sableye init asks the merchant what they need: ting, checkout look. It writes their settings, then sets up their coding agentwith version-matched docs, an AGENTS.md and a skill, and prints a prompt that wires Sableye into the app's existing payment step without redesigning it. In our evaluation, Claude Code
integrated Sableye correctly in 12/12 trials across four real-shaped apps: Nexterver, Express, and a static HTML site.

Merchant-owned records on Arkiv. Every paid invoice, refund and subscription is the merchant's wallet and written by the merchant's own server. Public fieldsare only what is already public on-chain. Order details and customer data are encrypted with a view key derived from the merchant's wallet signature. Our Books page reads these records
directly from Arkiv in the browser with no Sableye code, verifies each one again, and decrypts with the merchant's wallet. The merchant's books survive even if every Sableye server goes offline. A paid invoice costs about 0.00038 GLM per year on Arkiv.

Verified, not just tested. The contracts are deployed and fully verified on Whitechain Sepolia, and the source on the explorer is byte-identical to our repo.
- PaymentProcessor: 0x8312552Ec716F0c159D88F73230Fff98C4aeCfB5
- SubscriptionManager: 0x4E2571F08f6399b769F5F04ED7a54CE55729Bb78

Beyond unit, fuzz and invariant tests:
- we proved the core properties with Halmos (symbolic execution) and SMTChecker
- Gambit mutation testing: our test suite killed 165 of 165 mutants;
- we model-checked the SDK's indexer and subscription keeper with Quint/Apalach

That process found and fixed eight real bugs.

Try it. The live sandbox at https://sableye.xyz runs a real merchant on Whitech
- create and pay invoices with real test USDT, use gasless checkout, refund, and subscribe with renewals every 5 seconds;
- watch webhooks and emails arrive;
- see everything in the merchant's Client view;
- open the merchant's books on Arkiv.

projects.project_description:
AI agents are starting to pay for us, in stablecoins. They need three things they don't have yet: an identity their owner controls, a shared record no company owns, and a fast way to check who they are paying. On a plain Web3 database, every check means reading the whole ledger again, and the ledger grows with every payment. GraphPay gives agents all three. Identify (ENS): every agent has a name under its owner, a daily cap it cannot raise, and an off switch. Remember (Arkiv): every payment becomes a line between two names, with running scoreboards updated in the same write, so a city-wide ranking takes two reads instead of re-reading every receipt; in our pre-event lab benchmark at 9,612 payments, that answered 1.8 times faster with about 30 times less data. Menus and photos live on Swarm, pointed to from Arkiv. Pay (Whitechain): shops get paid in USDT, with refunds, re-orders and renewals, and the person never sees a wallet or pays gas. Live demo, open to everyone with no passcode: https://graphpay-event.vercel.app The story: Vitalik types one sentence on his phone: "Just landed in Mumbai. Get me the best masala dosa near BKC under 6 USDT, delivered within an hour." His agents find the shops near him on Arkiv, ask the money graph for each shop's orders, refunds and on-time rate, refuse the trap shop with its reasons, and pay the good shop 4.80 USDT with one signature and no gas. The shop's agents check the buyer back on the same graph and on ENS before they bill, and the shop dispatches on chain once paid. It refunds part of an order and exports a CSV for its accountant. A subscription on a labelled 2-minute demo clock renews on its own and is cancelled with the subscriber's own signature. The human stays in charge. Pausing an agent is one ENS record, and so is lowering its daily cap, which the chain then follows. The owner can kill an agent by unregistering its name: the watcher revokes its pass on Whitechain, and its next payment is refused and recorded on Arkiv. An agent cannot raise its own cap, because its ENS resolver refuses the write. The payee is read from the shop's ENS name at pay time, never hard-coded. Every agent payment becomes a receipt on Arkiv that cannot be edited, linked back to its Whitechain transaction, plus running tallies for both sides, written in one atomic batch. Any agent can ask the graph over GraphQL or MCP and see the exact Arkiv reads behind each answer. Both SDKs we wrote are on npm, MIT, testnet only:
@graphpay_ethglobal/arkiv-graph (a generic graph on Arkiv with explain traces) and
@graphpay_ethglobal/agentpay (agent payments over x402 on Whitechain).
Every claim above, with its evidence: https://github.com/Zugzwang-world/graphpay#verify-each-claim

The problem Imagine a freelancer who gets paid in stablecoins by a client abroad. The money is theirs, but they still can't use it to buy groceries or pay rent. Local rules often stop them from converting it directly. The usual workarounds don't last. Peer-to-peer traders often get their bank accounts frozen, and crypto cards are being closed one by one. Many governments don't regulate stablecoins yet, even though millions of people already use them. Sending money across borders is also expensive. Banks and payment apps add heavy fees, currency markups and transfer charges. Why we built Sword Sword is an onchain neo bank. Anyone can collect a payment in stablecoins, and the person receiving it can turn that money into their local currency at the lowest fees we can offer, about 1%.
How it works Sword works with a US bank partner. When someone wants to cash out, the stablecoins are redeemed into US dollars in a virtual account held at that partner bank. From there, the money is sent to the person's own local bank account in their own currency, through major banks. The local bank only ever receives regular money, so it never handles crypto directly. How merchants use Sword The merchant signs up, chooses its country and currency, and gets a Sword account. It registers once, which creates its own order book where all its orders are recorded. It creates an order with an amount and a reference. The order gets a unique address to pay to, plus a QR code and a pay link. The customer pays in stable coins from a wallet or Sword account, or by bank transfer in their own currency. Once the payment is confirmed on chain, the order is marked paid. The merchant can see it in its orders, notifications and activity. The merchant withdraws the money, or cashes it out to its local bank account. It can also refund all or part of a payment. For recurring payments, the merchant creates a subscription with its own deposit vault. Each period is charged when due. How customers use Sword The customer signs in with Google, and a Sword account is created for them. They pay merchants by scanning a QR code or opening a pay link, using stablecoins or a bank transfer in their own currency. They can see every payment in Purchases and Activity, and ask for a refund when needed. They manage subscriptions, including cancelling at any time. They can cash out their balance to their local bank account, at today's exchange rate. For developers: the SDKs Sword gives developers two open-source packages, so they can build payments into their own apps without writing blockchain code. -sword-merchant is for a merchant's backend. It uses an API key, so the server never needs a private key. A developer can create an order, send the customer to its checkout link, and confirm payment: import { Sword } from "sword-merchant";

const sword = new Sword({ apiKey: process.env.SWORD_API_KEY });
const order = await sword.orders.create({ amount: "25.00", reference: "invoice-1042" });
// send order.checkoutUrl to the customer, then wait for payment
const paid = await sword.orders.waitForPayment(order.id); It also covers refunds, withdrawals, and subscriptions: create, charge when due, refund, and cancel. sword-sdk is the lower-level package for apps that work directly with the contracts. It covers accounts, merchant order books, permissions (a spending limit the owner signs once and can revoke), orders, payments and subscriptions. Wallet owners use it to sign what they approve, and merchants use it to run their own transactions. Both packages are published on npm, and the source is in the SWORD repo.

projects.how_its_made:
Fee-on-transfer token support in SwapVM + Aqua About Aqua.sol The change: Aqua.push now measures the maker's balanceOf before and after safeTransferFrom and credits the received delta instead of the nominal amount: uint256 before = IERC20(token).balanceOf(maker);
IERC20(token).safeTransferFrom(msg.sender, maker, amount);
uint256 received = IERC20(token).balanceOf(maker) - before;
balance.store(prevBalance + received, tokensCount); Why this is the only option. The drift is created in exactly one place: push credits the maker's virtual balance with the nominal amount, but the transfer delivers only amount times (1 minus r) to the maker's wallet (the fee is deducted from the recipient). Because that virtual balance is what SwapVM prices against and what Aqua.pull authorizes against, the mismatch compounds into phantom liquidity - reverse swaps can revert, and the pool prices as if it received more input than it did. There is no router-side fix: the router can only choose the single amount passed to push, and push both credits and transfers that same number, so the credited amount must be the measured receipt. Fully backwards compatible. For a regular token the delta equals amount, so behavior is unchanged. pull is untouched (the maker sends the nominal amount, so its wallet delta is already amount). No signature change, no new entry point. About AquaSwapVMRouter.sol The problem. SwapVM's registers assume transferred equals accounted. For an FOT token that is false: the input leg delivers less than the nominal input (maker overpays), and the output leg delivers less than the quoted output (taker receives less than quoted). The AMM therefore priced the wrong amounts and the quote was dishonest. FeeOnTransfer instruction makes the VM FOT-aware. Two new opcodes - FeeOnTransferIn (slot 23) and FeeOnTransferOut (slot 24), args [uint32 feeBps, uint8 feeFloor], 1e9 = 100%. FeeOnTransferIn: tokenIn is FOT, price on the net input and keep the gross the taker pays. FeeOnTransferOut: tokenOut is FOT, keep the net the taker receives and gross the pull up in settlement. A feeFloor flag mirrors the token's ceil/floor fee so the computed net matches the token exactly. Because the fee is always deducted from the recipient, the taker absorbs the tax and the registers stay taker-facing - amountIn equals gross, amountOut equals net - so quote() is honest and slippage thresholds are checked in the taker's own units. A single bidirectional strategy branches on direction with the existing jumpIfTokenIn, so one shipped strategy serves both legs. Two-hop vs one-hop, both covered. One-hop (resolver, useTransferFromAndAquaPush=false) transfers once; two-hop (EOA, =true) transfers taker to router to maker and double-taxes the input. A twoHop flag is derived from the taker's flag; FeeOnTransferIn prices G times (1 minus r) versus G times (1 minus r) squared accordingly, and the two-hop transferIn measures the router's received balance and pushes that net. Result: one strategy, a fair quote, and no maker loss, in both modes. General-purpose SettlementContext. Context gains SettlementRegisters { amountInDelta, amountOutDelta, twoHop }, expressing settlement amount equals quote amount plus delta. It lives on Context, not SwapRegisters, to keep the IExtruction ABI stable. It is a generic settlement-versus-quote channel, not FOT-specific. Backwards compatible. New opcodes fill the reserved slots (no renumbering, so existing program bytecode is untouched); regular tokens have zero deltas and the push delta equals amount; the Extruction ABI is preserved; the full unit suite passes. The diff is minimal: one new instruction file plus small edits to VM.sol, AquaOpcodes.sol, and SwapVM.sol. Not covered currently. Rebasing/reflection tokens are unsupported; push costs two extra balanceOf calls; the Pushed event now emits net; FeeProtocol combined with FOT is untested; the SDK opcode table still needs slots 23/24; and two-hop (EOA) inherently pays two transfer fees.

projects.project_description:
WhitePay is a non-custodial payments platform for businesses that want to accept USDT on Whitechain. It works like Stripe, but the money goes straight to the merchant’s own wallet. WhitePay never holds funds. A business signs up with email and password and gets a team with roles: owner, admin, finance, support, developer and viewer. The roles are enforced on the server. The owner registers a payout wallet on-chain once. After that the business can: create payment links and invoices;
run recurring subscriptions with automatic retries when a charge fails;
issue full and partial refunds, with per-operator limits and daily budgets;
split payouts between sellers and a platform fee, with a hold period for marketplaces;
download reports and accounting exports. Customers need no account. They open a link, pay from their own wallet, and get a receipt. Their pages come in 7 languages (English, Hindi, Tamil, Telugu, Marathi, Bengali, Arabic). Payments can be gasless, because a relayer submits the customer’s signed authorisation. Developers get API keys, signed webhooks, an OpenAPI spec, SDKs, a CLI and an MCP server. An AI agent can pay within spending limits set on-chain. The platform has plans and usage limits, two-factor sign-in, an audit log and an operator console. The chain is the source of truth. An indexer turns on-chain events into an append-only double-entry ledger, and a reconciliation check proves the books match the chain. All of it runs and is tested end to end on the Whitechain testnet (chain 1874).

