projects.how_its_made_markdown:
Contracts are written in Solidity 0.8.26 with Foundry. The core is NidhiHook, a Uniswap v4 hook built on OpenZeppelin's uniswap-hooks BaseHook with exactly three permissions: beforeSwap, afterSwap, and afterSwapReturnDelta. The address flag bits are 0xC4, mined with HookMiner per chain.

Because sender inside a Uniswap v4 hook is the router rather than the user, NidhiSwapRouter stores msg.sender in EIP-1153 transient storage (tstore) before calling PoolManager.unlock(), and NidhiHook reads it via msgSender() (tload) to bind execution to mandate.executor.

beforeSwap decodes the grant from hookData and enforces 10 sequential checks:
- an EIP-712 mandate from an approver and an attestation from an auditor (ECDSA first, ERC-1271 fallback so a Safe or NidhiBountyBoard can hold the role);
- the two signers must be distinct and hold live roles via IGrantAuthority;
- the grantId has never been used, the deadline hasn't passed, and the swap is exact-input;
- the payee address matches live ENSv2 subname resolution for the destination chain;
- the daily spending cap;
- an optional reference-price guard reading a canonical pool's slot0 through StateView.

afterSwap then take()s the full output directly into NidhiDispatcher and returns +output as the hook delta, so the swapper receives zero tokens.

The hacky bit: a gated hook normally breaks quoting because V4Quoter simulates the swap without a signed mandate. We pass the chain's V4Quoter into the hook and let it through with a zero delta (the quoter reverts its own swap anyway), so the app quotes every grant with the real V4Quoter. Liquidity is minted as a DAO-owned NFT through PositionManager with Permit2 after we proved in a fork test that liquidity seeded via the v4 test router can be drained by anyone.

ENS: ENSv2Authority maps each role (approver, auditor, guardian) to an Enhanced Access Control (EAC) ROLE_SET_TEXT permission on nidhi-dao.eth's PermissionedResolver. resolveGrantee walks registry -> subregistry -> resolver and reads addr using the ENSIP-11 coin type of the destination chain. We tested three subname models: alice (shared resolver), bob (own resolver, DAO control renounced), and carol (expiring grant window). NidhiBountyBoard holds the approver role on-chain, and the agent's identity is auditor.nidhi-dao.eth.

CCIP: NidhiDispatcher sends a programmable token transfer (tokens + grant data) and maintains a forward-only ledger (NONE -> DISPATCHED -> HELD / SETTLED). DestinationReceiver decouples the payout and return ACK in separate try/catch blocks so a reverting transfer or depleted fee reserve never reverts ccipReceive. Chainlink Automation (checkUpkeep / performUpkeep, MAX_BATCH=10) automatically retries held payouts and pending ACKs. For chains without a direct lane, NidhiHopRelay forwards the token transfer across an intermediate chain and rewrites the messageId on the return receipt; we settled Base -> Arbitrum -> Unichain live.

For CCIP 2.0 Faster-Than-Finality, NidhiDispatcher encodes GenericExtraArgsV3 with a configurable block depth per lane, and DestinationReceiver opts in via getCCVsAndFinalityConfig strictly for its known source dispatcher. Probing the lanes live showed Sepolia -> Base and Sepolia -> Arbitrum require a 32-block depth, cutting settlement to 6m 36s and 7m 15s.

Agent & Frontend: The TypeScript/viem agent runs in GitHub Actions on PR merge. A deterministic rubric checks merge status, review count, green CI runs, file globs, and diff bounds, while a multi-LLM jury (Groq, Cerebras, Gemini, Claude) holds advisory veto power only. The React + Vite + wagmi/viem + TanStack Query frontend runs statically on GitHub Pages with a build-time snapshot and incremental log cache for sub-3s loads.

Testing & Security: 139 Foundry tests across 16 suites (unit, fuzz, and invariant tests proving zero residual balances, value conservation, and daily cap enforcement), Slither static analysis (38 findings triaged, 0 open High/Medium), live Sepolia fork tests, and mainnet fork rehearsals across 7 chains.
